---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import TableScroll from "../components/TableScroll.astro";
import Toc from "../components/Toc.astro";
import { stores } from "../data/stores";

const questions = {
  "delivered-total": "What is the delivered total?",
  "import-fees": "Why does it say import fees are not shown, and do I still pay duties?",
  "check-match": "What does “Check match” mean?",
  "stores-that-dont-ship": "Why can’t some stores be compared?",
  "other-sellers": "Why does a store’s result name another seller?",
  "exchange-rates": "Where do the exchange rates come from?",
  "delete-data": "How do I delete my data?",
  "delivery-location": "Why does another Amazon store’s “Deliver to” box now show my country?",
  cache: "Why did “Check again” come back instantly?",
  buy: "Does it buy anything or touch my cart?",
  affiliation: "Is it affiliated with Amazon, and does it earn commissions?",
  stores: "Which Amazon stores does it work on?",
  "background-tabs": "Why do tabs open during a check?",
  "robot-check": "What happens when a store shows a robot check?",
  "stay-here": "What does “Stay here” mean?",
} as const;

const tocItems = Object.entries(questions).map(([id, question]) => ({ href: `#${id}`, label: question }));
---

<Base
  title="FAQ"
  description="Answers about delivered totals, import fees, “Check match”, exchange rates, deleting your data and the stores Across Amazon checks."
>
  <PageIntro
    title="Frequently asked questions"
    lede="Short answers about delivered totals, matches, stores and the data the extension keeps."
  />
  <div class="container page-body">
    <Toc items={tocItems} />

    <article class="prose faq-answers">
      <h2 id="delivered-total">{questions["delivered-total"]}</h2>
      <p>
        The delivered total is the item price, plus shipping to your destination, plus the import fees deposit. Your
        destination is the country in Amazon’s “Deliver to” box on the page you are on.
      </p>
      <p>
        The extension reads each part as Amazon quotes it on the product page, in the currency the page shows. If Amazon
        displays a total that is higher than that sum, the displayed total is used.
      </p>
      <p>
        To rank the stores, each delivered total is converted to your display currency at the
        day’s <a href="#exchange-rates">exchange rate</a>. The page you are on is always the reference: the saving and
        the difference on each row are measured against its delivered total. Click a row in the ranked list to see each
        part in that store’s own currency.
      </p>

      <h2 id="import-fees">{questions["import-fees"]}</h2>
      <p>
        Amazon quotes import fees for an address abroad only on some pages. On amazon.com, for example, they are in its
        AmazonGlobal details. When a store’s page prices the item and the delivery but shows no import fees, its
        delivered total is the item price plus shipping. Any import charges collected on delivery are not included.
      </p>
      <p>The popup says so wherever that total appears:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The store’s row reads “incl. shipping, import fees not shown”.</li>
        <li>Its breakdown sheet shows “Not shown” for the import fees deposit.</li>
        <li>
          If it is the cheapest store, the verdict card reads “Item and shipping included. Amazon showed no import fees,
          so duties may still be due on delivery.”
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So yes, you may still pay duties. In that case the saving leaves out any duties collected on delivery, while the
        price on the page you are on may include them.
      </p>

      <h2 id="check-match">{questions["check-match"]}</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="nowrap">“Check match”</span> is a pill on a row in the ranked list, and on the verdict card when
        that row is the cheapest. It means the listing was found by searching and the extension is less than 90% sure it
        is the same product.
      </p>
      <p>
        A listing found under your product’s ASIN, Amazon’s own product identifier, is the same product. It counts as an
        exact match and never shows the pill. A listing found by searching is scored, not certain. Its confidence
        combines how closely the listing’s title, brand and variant match your product with how cleanly its product page
        could be read. A listing from your product’s brand with the same model number counts as a close title match even
        when the store words the title differently, but it usually still scores below 90% and so carries the pill.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before a listing found by searching is ranked, its page is checked for your colour, pack and size. When the page
        shows another variant but offers yours, the extension opens your variant’s page and reads that instead; when the
        page does not offer your variant, the store is reported as “No confident match”. Open the listing and check the
        variant before you buy.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you see the pill, also confirm that it is the same product in the same condition. Listings that score below
        the matching threshold are never ranked. They appear with
        the <a href="#stores-that-dont-ship">stores that could not be compared</a> as “No confident match”.
      </p>

      <h2 id="stores-that-dont-ship">{questions["stores-that-dont-ship"]}</h2>
      <p>
        Some stores will not compare, and that is expected. They are collapsed at the bottom of the results, under a
        line such as <span class="nowrap">“4 marketplaces could not be compared”</span>. Expand it to see each store’s
        status:
      </p>
      <TableScroll label="Why a store could not be compared">
        <table class="table-stack">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
              <th scope="col">What happened</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">No confident match</th>
              <td>
                The store does not list your product’s ASIN and no search result scored above the matching threshold.
                Or the closest listing is another variant, and its page does not offer yours. Nothing is compared rather
                than risking the wrong product.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Delivered total not quotable</th>
              <td>
                Amazon did not show the item price or the shipping for your destination, for example because the item
                cannot be shipped there. The note names the destination, for example: “Amazon did not show a delivered
                price to Switzerland.” When Amazon gives a reason, the note repeats it. When the page offered{" "}
                <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span> and did not say the item is currently unavailable,
                the extension read that list first, so this status then means that no seller there delivers the item
                new to your destination either, or that the list could not be read, and the note says which.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Could not read the page</th>
              <td>The product page could not be read, or the tab failed to load in time.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Could not set delivery location</th>
              <td>
                The store kept quoting delivery to another place because its delivery location could not be switched to
                your destination, for example because it does not offer that country. The note says which: “This store
                does not offer delivery to Switzerland.” or “Could not set this store’s delivery location to Switzerland,
                so its price is for another place.”
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableScroll>
      <p>
        You will see two of these most often. “Delivered total not quotable” usually means neither the store nor any
        seller it lists under <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span> delivers this item new to your
        country. “Could not set delivery location” means the store would not switch to, or does
        not offer, your delivery country. For example, amazon.com.au offers few countries. A store with that status is
        never ranked, because its price is for another place.
      </p>

      <h2 id="other-sellers">{questions["other-sellers"]}</h2>
      <p>
        A store’s own listing can show no price for your destination while other sellers on the same page deliver
        there. Amazon then shows <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span> instead of a price. The extension
        reads that list, up to three pages of it, as pressing the button would, and quotes the offer that is cheapest
        delivered among the new offers whose item and delivery prices (or free delivery) it could read and convert at
        the day’s <a href="#exchange-rates">exchange rate</a>. The row’s breakdown says who sells and dispatches the
        offer, as far as Amazon’s listing names them, and how many offers it was chosen from.
      </p>
      <p>
        Such a quote shows no import fees: the list prices the item and the delivery only, so duties may still be due
        on delivery. When the quoted offer is on the page you are on, the result card says that its price leaves out
        import fees. The offer is Amazon’s listing, not the extension’s. Open it
        with <span class="nowrap">“Open on …”</span> and check the seller before you buy.
      </p>

      <h2 id="exchange-rates">{questions["exchange-rates"]}</h2>
      <p>
        They are the European Central Bank (ECB) daily reference rates. When the ECB feed cannot be loaded, the extension
        gets the same rates from Frankfurter (api.frankfurter.dev) instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rates are normally downloaded once per UTC day and reused for the rest of that day. If a download fails, it
        is tried again on the next check. These requests carry no information about you or the product. Like any web
        request, they let those servers see your IP address. The popup’s footer shows the date of the rates in use.
        Until a check has loaded rates, it reads “ECB daily rates”.
      </p>
      <p>
        Totals are converted to your display currency, which you choose
        under <span class="nowrap">“Show prices in”</span> in the popup’s settings, or on the extension’s options page.
        Each store prices in its own currency, so the rate your bank or Amazon’s currency converter applies decides what
        you finally pay.
      </p>

      <h2 id="delete-data">{questions["delete-data"]}</h2>
      <p>
        Everything the extension keeps is in its local storage in your browser, and nothing it stores is sent to the
        developer or to anyone else. <a href="/how-it-works/#storage">How it works</a> lists what is kept and for how long.
      </p>
      <p>Removing the extension from Chrome deletes everything it stored. To clear its storage without removing it:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open <code>chrome://extensions</code>.</li>
        <li>Turn on Developer mode.</li>
        <li>Click “service worker” under Across Amazon.</li>
        <li>Run <code>chrome.storage.local.clear()</code> in the console that opens.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Removing the extension does not change back any Amazon store’s delivery location. Change it in that store’s
        “Deliver to” box. It also does not remove the visits it made from Chrome’s history or from your Amazon history.
        Clear those in Chrome and on Amazon.
      </p>
      <p>The full wording is in the privacy policy, under <a href="/privacy/#deleting-your-data">Deleting your data</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="delivery-location">{questions["delivery-location"]}</h2>
      <p>
        So that every store quotes delivery to the same place, a check may set each other store’s delivery location to
        the country in the “Deliver to” box of the page you started from. This is the same change as picking that
        country in the store’s own “Choose your location” box, and the store is left set that way after the check. For
        example, if you start on amazon.com with “Deliver to Switzerland”, the “Deliver to” box on amazon.de, amazon.fr
        and the other stores it checks then shows Switzerland.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only a country is set, never an address. The store you started from is never changed. Your own tabs on a changed
        store show the new location after their next reload. To change a store back, pick another location in its
        “Deliver to” box. <a href="/how-it-works/#delivery-location">How it works</a> explains the change in full.
      </p>
      <p>
        A store whose box shows your country may still not ship your item there. When its page offers{" "}
        <span class="nowrap">“See All Buying Options”</span> and does not say the item is currently unavailable, the
        check reads that list too, and when no seller there delivers it new either, lists the store as{" "}
        <span class="nowrap">“Delivered total not quotable”</span>. A store that does not offer your country, or whose change Amazon refuses, keeps quoting its
        previous place and is listed as “Could not set delivery location”. Both appear with
        the <a href="#stores-that-dont-ship">stores that could not be compared</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cache">{questions.cache}</h2>
      <p>
        A finished check is reused for 30 minutes. An older result is deleted only when exactly the same check runs
        again; otherwise it stays until you remove the extension or clear its storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you press <span class="nowrap">“Check again”</span> within those 30 minutes, for the same product, variant,
        destination, “Deliver to” text and set of stores, you get the stored result back at once, and no tabs open.
        After 30 minutes a fresh check runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        A stored result includes the “Deliver to” text and the address of the product page. To remove it sooner,
        see <a href="#delete-data">how to delete your data</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="buy">{questions.buy}</h2>
      <p>
        No. It never adds to cart, checks out or buys anything. <span class="nowrap">“Open on …”</span> opens that
        store’s listing in a new tab. You review it and decide whether to buy it there, on Amazon.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your saved addresses and cart are not touched, and it never reads your passwords, your orders or your cookies.
        It does change the <a href="#delivery-location">delivery location of the other stores</a> it checks.
      </p>

      <h2 id="affiliation">{questions.affiliation}</h2>
      <p>No. Across Amazon is not affiliated with or endorsed by Amazon.</p>
      <p>
        It earns no commission either. Every <span class="nowrap">“Open on …”</span> link is the store’s plain product
        page address, with no affiliate or referral code added. The extension has no analytics and no server of its own,
        and nothing is sent to the developer.
      </p>

      <h2 id="stores">{questions.stores}</h2>
      <p>Product pages on these ten Amazon stores:</p>
      <TableScroll label="Supported Amazon stores">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Store</th>
              <th scope="col">Domain</th>
              <th scope="col">Currency</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              stores.map((store) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{store.country}</th>
                  <td>{store.domain}</td>
                  <td class="nowrap">{store.currency}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableScroll>
      <p>
        A check covers every store switched on in the settings except the one you are on, so by default the other nine.
        Turn stores on or off under <span class="nowrap">“Marketplaces to check”</span> in the popup’s settings, or on
        the extension’s options page. Fewer stores make the check faster.
      </p>
      <p>
        On any other site, or on an Amazon page that is not a product page, such as search results or the cart, the
        popup asks you to open an Amazon product page and the button stays disabled.
      </p>

      <h2 id="background-tabs">{questions["background-tabs"]}</h2>
      <p>
        The extension reads each store’s prices from that store’s own pages, as Amazon shows them. So it opens those
        pages one store at a time, in background tabs that never take focus, and closes each tab as soon as it has been
        read. A check takes about 3 seconds per store.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pages it opens are ordinary visits: they appear in Chrome’s history and, if you are signed in to Amazon, may
        appear in your Amazon browsing and search history. They are made by your browser with your own Amazon session and
        may also influence Amazon’s recommendations.
      </p>
      <p>
        <span class="nowrap">“Stop checking”</span> cancels the run and closes any background tabs it opened. Closing
        the popup does not stop a check: it finishes in the background. Reopen the popup on the same tab to follow its
        progress.
      </p>

      <h2 id="robot-check">{questions["robot-check"]}</h2>
      <p>
        A store that shows a robot check may report “No confident match” or “Could not read the page”. It is then listed
        with the <a href="#stores-that-dont-ship">stores that could not be compared</a> and is not ranked. If
        you <a href="/support/#report">report a problem</a>, say whether that store shows a robot check when you open
        it yourself.
      </p>

      <h2 id="stay-here">{questions["stay-here"]}</h2>
      <p>
        It means no other store beat the delivered total on the page you are on. The verdict card turns slate and
        reads <span class="nowrap">“Stay here”</span>. The page you are on is the highlighted top row, and every other
        row shows how much more it would cost.
      </p>
      <p>The card has other answers too:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The cheapest delivered total with no saving, when the price on the page you are on could not be read. The
          popup still names the cheapest store but has nothing to compare it with.
        </li>
        <li>No delivered price, when none of the stores could quote a delivered price to your destination.</li>
        <li>
          <span class="nowrap">“Couldn’t compare”</span>, with the reason, when the check itself fails, for example
          because the exchange rates could not be loaded.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="faq-more">
        Not answered here? The <a href="/support/">support page</a> explains how to ask the developer.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .faq-answers h2 {
    margin-top: 1.75em;
    padding-top: 1.25em;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .faq-answers h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
    border-top: 0;
  }

  .faq-answers th[scope="row"] {
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .faq-more {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
